rspend of \u00a36.39 million\u2014representing 97 per cent of the approved budget\u2014suggests either the project was severely delayed into the following financial year, the budget was drastically misjudged, or the work was not undertaken as planned. The council has not explained the discrepancy in the papers.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"Risk Register - 11 New Road Disposal (1)","paper_url":"https://mycouncil.oxford.gov.uk/documents/s92123/92123.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Oxford Council spent just \u00a3169k of \u00a36.56m fire safety budget for tower blocks in 2025/26","lead":"Oxford City Council budgeted \u00a36.56 million in 2025/26 for replacing fire alarm systems in its tower blocks, mandatory work under fire safety regulations. It spent \u00a3169,080 \u2014 a 97 per cent underspend. The scale of the variance, revealed in the council's financial outturn report to Cabinet this month, raises questions about whether the work was delayed into the current year or the budget was drastically overestimated. The council's housing stock includes a number of high-rise residential buildings where fire safety compliance is a legal requirement. No explanation was provided in the outturn report for why such a substantial safety project remained largely unspent when the year closed.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"Risk Register - 11 New Road Disposal (1)","paper_url":"https://mycouncil.oxford.gov.uk/documents/s92123/92123.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Oxford delays fire safety work on tower blocks, spending just \u00a3169,000 of \u00a36.5m budget","lead":"Oxford City Council has spent only \u00a3169,080 of a \u00a36.56 million budget allocated for fire alarm system replacement on its tower blocks in the year to March 2026, a 97 per cent underspend on mandatory safety compliance work. The replacement programme is required to meet fire safety regulations. The council's financial outturn report, presented to Cabinet on 16 September, does not explain the shortfall \u2014 whether the project has been delayed into the current year, the budget was radically miscalculated, or work has not proceeded as planned. The scale of the variance between budget and actual spend raises questions about progress on a safety-critical project that affects residents in the council's highest-risk buildings.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"Risk Register - 11 New Road Disposal (1)","paper_url":"https://mycouncil.oxford.gov.uk/documents/s92123/92123.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"TM Annual Report 25-26 v2 (1)","paper_url":"https://mycouncil.oxford.gov.uk/documents/s92146/92146.pdf","headline":"Lothbury property fund investment showing \u00a30.826m realised loss with \u00a30.953m total projected loss","detail":"Of the council's \u00a37m investment in CCLA's Lothbury property fund (made in April 2013), \u00a36.864m has been redeemed at a value of \u00a36.028m as at 31 March 2026, producing a realised loss of \u00a30.826m. The council estimates a further loss of \u00a30.953m overall once remaining assets are disposed. The paper notes this loss is 'mitigated by an earmarked reserve being held to cover this risk' and the fund has paid \u00a32.4m in dividends, but the net position remains negative.","category":"reserves","matters":"This shows capital risk on the council's non-treasury investments and that reserves are being consumed to cover losses on long-term holdings, reducing available reserves for service pressures.","figures":"Initial investment April 2013: \u00a37m; redeemed to 31 March 2026: \u00a36.028m; realised loss: \u00a30.826m; total projected loss: \u00a30.953m; dividends received: \u00a32.4m","quote":"As at the 31st March 2026, \u00a36.864 million of our initial \u00a37 million investment had been redeemed at a value of \u00a36.028 million, a loss of \u00a30.826 million on the initial investment value. It is estimate that there will be a \u00a30.953 million capital loss overall once all of the remaining funds are returned.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"TM Annual Report 25-26 v2 (1)","paper_url":"https://mycouncil.oxford.gov.uk/documents/s92146/92146.pdf","headline":"Council maintains \u00a3188.7m of under-borrowing (internal borrowing plus investments), creating refinancing risk if cash balances fall","detail":"Against a Capital Financing Requirement of \u00a3458.5m, Oxford holds only \u00a3288.5m of external debt, leaving \u00a3169.9m of internal borrowing (cash funding capital and operations). The paper explicitly notes 'internal borrowing is still preferable to borrowing externally as the interest rate payable on an external loan is much higher than that which can be earnt on investments'. However, this assumes cash remains available. The paper states: 'If the net borrowing position, interest rate position and/or CFR changed significantly, the prospect of taking on additional debt would be reviewed'.","category":"borrowing","matters":"If the council's cash balance is drawn down by capital spending, deficit, or investment losses, it will be forced to external borrow at 3.41%+ rather than the 3.75% base rate it currently earns internally, creating budget pressure.","figures":"CFR \u00a3458.466m; external debt \u00a3288.528m; internal borrowing \u00a3169.938m; under-borrowing and investments \u00a3188.685m","quote":"In the current climate, internal borrowing is still preferable to borrowing externally as the interest rate payable on an external loan is much higher than that which can be earnt on investments.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"TM Annual Report 25-26 v2 (1)","paper_url":"https://mycouncil.oxford.gov.uk/documents/s92146/92146.pdf","headline":"Capital programme underspent by \u00a382.9m (46%), creating future refinancing need for slipped schemes","detail":"Oxford budgeted \u00a3178.5m of capital expenditure in 2025/26 but spent only \u00a395.6m, with HRA capital particularly hit (\u00a3141.1m budgeted, \u00a369.9m spent). The paper states this represents 'slippage in the programme, the resources for which will be moved into funding the expenditure in future financial years'. Currently the council funds planned capital through internal borrowing (cash balances), but if schemes complete in future years, it will need to either draw those balances or externally borrow.","category":"capital","matters":"A lender needs to know the council can either complete the \u00a382.9m of unspent capital schemes through internal cash without compromising liquidity, or will return to the market for external borrowing if cash is consumed elsewhere.","figures":"Capital budgeted \u00a3178.514m, actual \u00a395.596m (underspend \u00a382.918m); HRA capital budgeted \u00a3141.126m, actual \u00a369.912m (underspend \u00a371.214m)","quote":"Much of the variation to the original budget relates to slippage in the programme, the resources for which will be moved into funding the expenditure in future financial years.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"2025-26 Financial Outturn Report DRAFT v2.1 (1)","paper_url":"https://mycouncil.oxford.gov.uk/documents/s92095/92095.pdf","headline":"Awaab's Law and housing regulator compliance created \u00a31.214m damp and mould overspend mid-year","detail":"Following Awaab's Law coming into force in October 2025, damp and mould remediation emerged as a priority and the service spent \u00a32.014m against a budget of \u00a30.800m, creating a \u00a31.214m overspend. Separately, asbestos compliance overspent by \u00a30.365m due to new housing regulator standards, and gas maintenance overspent by \u00a30.141m to ensure compliance with updated housing regulations. These are mandatory compliance costs that the council must fund but has limited control over.","category":"other","matters":"A lender needs to understand the scale and likely trajectory of compliance costs driven by changing housing regulator standards, which are mandatory expenditures competing with capital and other services.","figures":"Damp and mould overspend \u00a31.214m; asbestos compliance overspend \u00a30.365m; gas maintenance overspend \u00a30.141m; total compliance overspend \u00a31.720m","quote":"Damp and Mould Works \u2013 this the most significant category of overspend at \u00a31.214m compared to the 2025/26 budget. This emerged as a priority in year following the introduction of \"Awaab's Law\" which came into force in October","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"2025-26 Financial Outturn Report DRAFT v2.1 (1)","paper_url":"https://mycouncil.oxford.gov.uk/documents/s92095/92095.pdf","headline":"Dwelling acquisitions delayed with rent income falling \u00a31.438m short of budget","detail":"Dwelling rent income underachieved budget by \u00a31.438m primarily due to delays in handover and occupation of new homes being acquired into the HRA. The council is pursuing housing supply expansion as part of its homelessness mitigation strategy, but the acquisition programme is underperforming, reducing rental income and limiting the council's ability to offset deficits in the HRA.","category":"income","matters":"A lender needs to know whether the council's housing acquisition programme is viable and whether delays will persistently worsen the HRA's structural deficit.","figures":"Dwelling rent underspend \u00a31.438m","quote":"Dwelling Rent underachieving the budget by \u00a31.438m, due mainly to the timings of the acquisition of new homes, i.e. delays in the handover and subsequent occupation of properties into the HRA, which generate rental income compared to those homes originally budgeted to be let in year","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-16"},"name":"Oxford","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-21","since_iso":"2026-08-22","distress":true,"headline":"Lothbury property fund investment showing \u00a30.826m realised loss with \u00a30.953m total projected loss","lead":"This shows capital risk on the council's non-treasury investments and that reserves are being consumed to cover losses on long-term holdings, reducing available reserves for service pressures.","also":["46 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Scrutiny Committee on 2026-10-13","n_findings":47,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"TM Annual Report 25-26 v2 (1)","top_paper_url":"https://mycouncil.oxford.gov.uk/documents/s92146/92146.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":117,"news_drafts_total":3}}